actors: Browser | Server | Policy evaluator

step: The caller asks for one specific note
msg: Browser -> Server: GET /notes/42
text: Unlike the admin panel, the answer depends on which note this is. A
  role alone can't decide it.

step: The server gathers both sides of the question
note: Server: caller row + note's owner_id
text: It needs the caller (id and role) and the resource (the note and who
  owns it). Neither comes from anything the request supplied, apart from the
  note's id.

step: The evaluator walks its rules in order
msg: Server -> Policy evaluator: evaluate(subject, note, read)
text: Rules are checked top to bottom: `admin-full-access`, then
  `owner-full-access`, then `default-deny`. The first one that matches
  decides.

step: The owner rule compares caller and note
note: Policy evaluator: _is_owner()
text: This is the relationship check, and the function you fix in this lab.
  It has to compare the caller to the note, because that is the whole
  difference between "your note" and "any note".

step: The decision names the rule that fired
msg: Policy evaluator -> Server: allow/deny + rule_id
text: Every call returns something concrete, an outcome and the id of the
  rule behind it. `default-deny` matches unconditionally at the end, so
  there is never a "no answer".

step: The rule id travels to the audit log and the browser
msg: Server -> Browser: 200 or 403 + rule_id
text: The same rule id is written to the audit log and returned to the
  trace panel. When you test this lab, read the rule id first and the
  status code second.
